COP8C                                                            Page 1  of 11
                                 COP8C FEATURES

  The COP8C Code Development System has the following features:

    þ Complete C compiler (real and protected mode compilers included).

    þ Built in macro cross-assembler.

    þ Expert system optimizations.

    þ Consistency checks between target processor and source code.

    þ Listing files include C source, assembler and generated object code.

    þ Separately compiled objects and libraries.

    þ Built-in linker.


@
COP8C                                                            Page 2  of 11
                       RUNNING COP8C FROM THE IDE MENU

  To run the COP8C compiler from the IDE main menu, select Option
  Selection and check these options:

    þ Assembler/Compiler    Should be COP8C.EXE (may include the path).

    þ Source File           The name of the source file you want to compile.

    þ Error File, Cross-    Select Yes for each of these files you want to
      Reference File, List  generate.  Move the cursor opposite the file name
      File, Object File     and press <Enter> until the word "Yes" appears.

    þ Hex Dump File         Press <Enter> to select: None, ASCII, Intel, S1,
                            S2, S3, INHX8M, INHX8S, INHX16, INHX32.

    þ Command Line Options  Other command line options for the COP8C.


@
COP8C                                                            Page 3  of 11
                            RUNNING COP8C FROM DOS

  The COP8C compiler can be run from the DOS command line by typing

    COP8C [sourcefile [options]]     (real mode compiler)
    COP8CP [sourcefile [options]]    (protected mode compiler)

  If you omit the sourcefile, the COP8C compiler will prompt you for:

    þ Source File:          Name of the file to be compiled.
    þ Error File:           Select Yes for sourcefile.ERR or type new name.
    þ Cross Reference File: Select Yes for sourcefile.XRF or type new name.
    þ Listing File:         Select Yes for sourcefile.LST or type new name.
    þ Hex Dump Type         Select one of None, ASCII, Intel, S1, S2, S3,
                            INHX8M, INHX8S, INHX16, INHX32.
    þ Object File:          Select Yes for sourcefile.OBJ or type new name.
    þ Call Macro Assembler: Select Yes to run macro assembler on source file.


@
COP8C                                                            Page 4  of 11
                      RUNNING COP8C FROM DOS (continued)

  DOS command line options:
    +da  ASCII hex format dump file    +di  Intel hex format dump file
    +ds  S1 hex format dump file       -d   No dump file            (default)
    +e   Generate error file           -e   No error file           (default)
    +i   Use 16-bit ints.              -i   Use 8-bit ints.         (default)
    +l   Generate listing file         -l   No listing file         (default)
    +m   Run the built-in macro        -m   Assumes sourcefile is C (default)
         assembler on sourcefile
    +o   Generate object file          -o   No object file          (default)
    +x   Generate cross-ref file       -x   No cross-reference file (default)
    c=<codename>    Renames the code file to <codename>.
    e=<errname>     Renames the error file to <errname>.
    l=<listname>    Renames the list file to <listname>.
    o=<objectname>  Renames the object file to <objectname>.
    x=<xrefname>    Renames the cross reference file <xrefname>.
    v=<video>       Change video mode to <video> (CGA, EGA, VGA or MONO).

@
COP8C                                                            Page 5  of 11
                             COP8C C EXTENSIONS

  The COP8C implementation has the following extensions to ANSI C:

    þ Binary constants (eg: 0b01101010 or 0B01101010).

    þ bits predefined struct allows single bit manipulations.  Declare as:
      bits bitname;  To access, use constants: bitname.0 or bitname.5

    þ Superset of the case statement, including
        ù case 4,5          multiple cases separated by commas
        ù case '0'..'9'     range of cases
        ù case var          variable case -- Use with caution!

    þ Processor-specific functions:
        ù NOP();    ù STOP();

continued on next page...

@
COP8C                                                            Page 6  of 11
                       COP8C C EXTENSIONS (continued)

    þ Address fixing.  A variable or function can be fixed to a specific
      location in memory with the @ operator.  (eg: int i @ 0x50;)

    þ #pragma preprocessor directives are used to describe the target
      processor to the compiler.

    þ __STARTUP() function to perform initialization on reset.

    þ Assemble single instruction with one of
      ù #asm (instruction);
      ù #asm <instruction>;
      ù #asm "instruction";

    þ defined(name) function which returns TRUE if "name" appears in the
      symbol table.


@
COP8C                                                            Page 7  of 11
                             COP8C C RESTRICTIONS

  COP8C has the following restrictions because of the COP8 architecture:

    þ Expression evaluation is limited to using the Accumulator and the B
      and X registers.  Temporary locations are used by the compiler only
      for 16-bit operations.

    þ Arguments to functions are limited to 2 bytes (using AC and X).

    þ Arrays are limited to a maximum of 112 bytes.  Multidimensional
      arrays and arrays of structs or unions are not allowed.

    þ Local variables have local scope (the same name may be used in
      different functions) but are stored globally (RAM space is not reused).




@
COP8C                                                            Page 8  of 11
                           PREPROCESSOR DIRECTIVES

  The COP8C compiler supports the following C directives:
    þ #asm              þ #if               þ #elif             þ #include
    þ #endasm           þ #ifdef            þ #else             þ #pragma
    þ #define           þ #ifndef           þ #endif            þ #error
    þ #undef

  #pragma directives:

    þ #pragma has <instruction-set>;
        Enables one of several instruction sets.  Replace <instruction-set>
        with 880, 820 or 840 (for COP880, COP820 or COP840 respectively).

    þ #pragma memory <special> [<size>] @ <location>;
        Describes the processor memory.  <special> can be RAM or ROM.

continued on next page...

@
COP8C                                                             Page 9 of 11
                     PREPROCESSOR DIRECTIVES (continued)

    þ #pragma option <compiler_directive>;   <compiler_directive> is one of:
        +c or -c    Assembler C compatibility.                      default +c
        +d or -d    Show disassembled code in listing file.         default +d
        e nnn       Maximum number of error messages before abort.  default 20
        f nnn       Form (page) length for listing file.            default 66
        +i or -i    Initialize ALL page 0 variables to 0 on reset.  default -i
        +l or -l    Turn listing on or off.                         default +l
        +m or -m    Assembler: assign RAM at #pragma memory         default +m
                    locations or at program counter.
        n notice    Set notice string in binary code file (.COD).
        +o          Generate modified listing file.
        p           Skip to next page in listing file.
        s0          Generate generic symbol table file (.SYM).
        s1          Generate generic symbol table file (.SYM).

continued on next page...

@
COP8C                                                            Page 10 of 11
                     PREPROCESSOR DIRECTIVES (continued)

        t title     Set title line for listing file pages.
        +u or -u    Use unsigned or signed chars.                   default -u
        v           Generate verbose symbol table information in
                    binary code file.  Must be FIRST line in file.
        +x or -x    Expand assembler macros.                        default -x

    þ #pragma portxx <name> @ <location>[ = initializer];
        Declares a port at the specified location with an optional initializer.
        Replace "xx" with r (read-only), w (write-only) or rw (read-write).

    þ #pragma regxx <name>;
        Declares a variable as a register.  "xx" is one of "a" (Accumulator),
        "b" (B register) "psw" (Program Status Word) or "x" (X Register).

continued on next page...

@
COP8C                                                            Page 11 of 11
                     PREPROCESSOR DIRECTIVES (continued)

    þ #pragma stack [<size>] @ <location>;
       Reserves <size> bytes at <location> for the stack.

    þ #pragma vector <name> @ <location>;
       Put interrupt vector <name> at <location>.  To make a function into an
       interrupt handler, call it <name>.











